---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const objects = await getCollection('objects', ({ data }) => !data.draft);
---

<Base title="About Sekiya" description="A place for computer science and computational experimentation." objectCount={objects.length}>
  <header class="page-head shell">
    <p class="kicker">About Sekiya</p>
    <h1>A place to leave a question open for a while.</h1>
    <p class="page-sub">
      Sekiya is an independent place for computer science and computational experimentation. It is for
      elegant experiments, strange behaviour, complexity made visible, and systems built simply to be
      understood. Serious work, without the stiff collar.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="institute-grid">
      <div class="reveal">
        <h3>What starts it</h3>
        <p>
          Curiosity. A question is enough to begin; the rest has to earn its place.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal">
        <h3>Areas</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Models &amp; Statistics — uncertain, delayed, and slightly unruly systems</li>
          <li>Systems &amp; Security — traces, hidden state, failure, and recovery</li>
          <li>Agentic Systems — autonomous software with some explaining to do</li>
          <li>Computation — simulation and visual machinery for complex behaviour</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal">
        <h3>What stays</h3>
        <p>
          The useful parts: the code, the limits, the failures, and enough of the path that someone else
          can see where the result came from.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal">
        <h3>Public work</h3>
        <p>
          Public work is linked. Private work is named on the index, but stays private until it is ready.
          One useful tool is
          <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/trace-npm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">trace-npm</a>
          {' '}— a small instrument for seeing what an npm lifecycle script actually did.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal">
        <h3>Elsewhere</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://joshuadefreitas.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Joshua de Freitas</a>
          {' '}keeps a personal index of projects, writing, and other experiments.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
